<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">1383–1385 Portuguese interregnum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1383–1385</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as 1383–1385 crisis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1383–1385 Portuguese interregnum was a succession crisis and civil war in Portugal after the death of King Ferdinand I without a male heir. His daughter Beatrice was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a>, but many Portuguese backed John, Master of Aviz; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> proclaimed him king in 1385, and his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> at Aljubarrota that year secured the throne and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1383-1385-portuguese-interregnum.jpg" alt="1383–1385 Portuguese interregnum" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean d'Wavrin (Chronique d'Angleterre) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalha_de_Aljubarrota_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
